From f3027dcaaca836b721961476115add9f6a7b702d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Travis Ellett <1130469+tellett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 19:20:56 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Point remote analysis caching at an external cache service

Bazel can serialize its Skyframe analysis graph and read it back, but outside
Google nothing can store it: SerializationModule hard-wires an in-memory
FingerprintValueStore, RemoteAnalysisCacheClient has no implementation, and
neither does LongVersionGetter, whose absence crashes upload outright. This adds
the three. With --experimental_remote_analysis_cache unset the server behaves
exactly as before.

Everything is driven by RemoteAnalysisCachingOptions, including the three knobs
that were already there and had no effect on anything: max_batch_size, deadline
and the new reader batch size now reach the RPCs they name. Two options are
backported from mainline, where they live in RemoteAnalysisCachingServicesOptions
-- reader_max_batch_size and max_in_flight_read_requests keep upstream's names,
defaults and help text, differing only in accessor style, mainline having since
moved these classes to abstract getters.

One option has no mainline equivalent and says so where it is declared: the read
linger exists because this implementation batches on a timer where mainline
bounds in-flight requests. Nothing else is added to the option surface. In
particular there is no namespace or tenant flag -- the service resolves the
tenant from the caller's credentials, and a client that could name a namespace
could name someone else's. Nothing else needs separating, because
FrontierNodeVersion already salts every top-level key with the install MD5 and
the top-level configuration checksum, and shared values are addressed by a
fingerprint of bytes the codec registry produced.

Authenticating adds no option at all. The channel is built by
GoogleAuthUtils.newChannel and its per-call auth comes from
GoogleAuthUtils.newCredentials, which is the same path --remote_cache takes:
--credential_helper, then .netrc, then --google_credentials. The lookup cache is
the remote module's own, so a helper runs once per URI per build rather than
once per consumer, and TLS follows the target's scheme exactly as it does for
the remote cache. A client already configured to reach a remote cache needs
nothing new to reach this.

Both directions are batched, because both of Bazel's paths move one entry at a
time: a 212,000-target upload makes roughly 600,000 put calls and a download that hits
makes about 5,000 lookups. The read linger, not the size cap, decides read batch
size in practice. Sweeping it on a cold worktree, 4,988 lookups each run:

   linger   wall   RPCs   mean keys
     1ms    6.7s    293        17.0
     5ms    7.1s    127        39.3
    25ms    6.5s     32       155.9
   100ms    7.5s     27       184.7

Past 25ms the round trips stop falling while the wait costs more than it saves.
Unbatched, the same download issued 4,988.

Batching on the return of an outstanding RPC instead, which mainline's
target-concurrency option and absent linger suggest it does, was built and
measured and is worse here. Skyframe's reads are a closed loop: the demand for
the next key is generated by a node the previous response unblocked, so the
queue cannot fill while the RPC that would fill it is outstanding. It issued 1.6
to 5 times more RPCs at every injected latency, with no wall-time difference.

Both read paths share one queue. They can, because the invalidation-data header
that one of them needs removed is now removed on the client: the value store
also fetches nested shared values that never had a header, so the distinction
belongs to the reader rather than to the request or the server. Sharing is what
makes the batches full.

Deadlines are applied per call rather than on the stored stub, where
withDeadlineAfter is absolute from the moment the stub is made and would fail
every RPC in a build that outlived it. Message size limits are raised in both
directions: a full read batch is well past gRPC's 4 MiB default, and exceeding
it fails the batch with RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED rather than degrading to a miss.

A third assumption surfaces only with external repositories, and is fixed rather
than conceded. RemoteConfiguredTargetValue.getTransitivePackages returns null --
they are never serialized -- while
shouldStoreTransitivePackagesInLoadingAndAnalysis tracks allowExternalRepositories.
Inside Google that is false and the two never meet; under bzlmod it is always
true, so the first cached dependency reaches
NestedSetBuilder.addTransitive(null) and crashes analysis. The three sites that
aggregate the value now skip a null, matching how BuildDriverFunction already
treats the same null. The aggregate then omits packages from cached subgraphs,
which under-reports symlink planting and repo-mapping manifests rather than
crashing.

Two assumptions in the analysis caching code do not hold outside Google and are
worked around here rather than solved:

FileDependencySerializer rejects absolute symlinks, but an open-source output
base is built from them -- every repository served from the repository contents
cache is reached by one, as is external/rules_java++toolchains+local_jdk/lib.
They are treated as unresolvable leaves when the cache is configured, which
loses the dependency edge on whatever they point at.

LongVersionGetter answers when a path last changed, and inside Google that comes
from the version control snapshot. The implementation here reports every path as
unchanged, which disables invalidation entirely. That is sound only when the
uploading and downloading workspaces hold identical content, so the service
refuses to start without an explicit acknowledgement. A real implementation has
to derive versions from something that tracks content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
